<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Virpur State</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Virpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Jadeja Rajput state in central Kathiawar whose town is now a major pilgrimage centre of the saint Jalaram.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Virpur, also called Virpur-Kherdi, was held by a branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadeja">Jadeja Rajputs</a> related to the ruling houses of Nawanagar and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gondal-state">Gondal</a>. It emerged as a separate holding around 1600.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Thakor ruled a small agrarian territory, paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaekwads">Gaekwads of Baroda</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/junagadh-state">Junagadh</a> before British supervision in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar-agency">Kathiawar Agency</a> after 1807. In the 19th century the town became the home of the saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalaram">Jalaram</a>, whose free kitchen drew devotees from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Virpur merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saurashtra-state">United State of Saurashtra</a> in 1948 and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1960. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalaram">Jalaram</a> temple in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rajkot-state">Rajkot</a> district keeps the town well known.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 1600 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">princely state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.54%;width:6.93%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Virpur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Virpur-Kherdi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1774&amp;focus=virpur-state" aria-label="Virpur State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6642 1322 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Virpur State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6962 1578L7364 1601M6962 1578L7364 1602M6962 1578L6896 1610"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6896 1610h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6962 1578h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6962 1578h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Virpur. <a href="/atlas?year=1774&amp;focus=virpur-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
